      "claim": "By November 1996 the prohibition against recruiting children under the age of fifteen into armed forces or groups had crystallised into customary international law.",
      "reasoning": "Customary law is formed by widespread and consistent state practice accompanied by opinio juris. By the mid‑1990s a substantial number of states had enacted criminal statutes or military regulations expressly prohibiting recruitment of children under fifteen, and the United Nations Committee on the Rights of the Child repeatedly affirmed that such recruitment violated international obligations. This combination of practice and belief in a legal duty satisfies the elements of customary law, establishing the prohibition as a norm of customary international humanitarian law by the relevant date.",
      "citations": []
    },
