      "claim": "The customary prohibition against recruiting children under fifteen creates individual criminal responsibility for persons who enlist or use them in hostilities.",
      "reasoning": "Under customary international humanitarian law, grave breaches of the Geneva Conventions and their Additional Protocols constitute war crimes for which individuals may be held criminally liable. The recruitment of children under fifteen is treated as a grave breach in the practice of international tribunals and in the commentary to the Geneva Conventions, thereby giving rise to individual criminal responsibility independent of any later treaty codification.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The protection of children under fifteen in armed conflict is a peremptory norm (jus cogens), and violations such as child recruitment give rise to individual criminal responsibility under customary law.",
      "reasoning": "Jus cogens norms are binding on all states and give rise to universal jurisdiction and individual liability. The international community has repeatedly recognised the protection of children from participation in hostilities as a fundamental value, reflected in the Convention on the Rights of the Child and the Additional Protocols, and affirmed in the practice of international courts. Consequently, recruiting children under fifteen triggers individual criminal responsibility as a breach of a peremptory norm, even before the 1998 Rome Statute codified the crime.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The argument that the norm was not customary because it was not yet criminalised in treaty law until 1998 fails, because customary law does not require universal criminalisation, only sufficient state practice and opinio juris, both of which existed by 1996.",
      "reasoning": "Customary international law is not dependent on treaty codification; it arises from consistent state conduct and a sense of legal obligation. By 1996 many states had already criminalised child recruitment, and international bodies had expressed that such recruitment was unlawful. This satisfies the customary law test, rendering the lack of a universal treaty prohibition irrelevant to the existence of individual criminal responsibility at that time.",
      "citations": []
    }
